  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Choose one next planning step using the local decision below. Return exactly one listed statement and stop. No other planning choices have been supplied.\n{\"context\": \"Section leaders can guide brief practice in separate corners, and all singers return to the full choir within the allocated rehearsal block.\", \"decision\": \"How should short sectional practice be organized?\", \"goal\": \"Make rehearsals easy to attend and prepare for while helping singers use shared rehearsal time effectively.\", \"problem\": \"A community choir is preparing a small program through a series of rehearsals. It has a conductor, willing section leaders, a booking organizer, numbered scores that members may use, and shared practice recordings. The rehearsal room has movable chairs and enough space for short sectional work. Members have varied experience and availability. Musical direction remains with the conductor, and each routine below can operate within any agreed rehearsal schedule.\"}\nCANDIDATE STATEMENTS:\nThe conductor works with one section while the other section leaders guide their own assigned passages in separate corners. The part needing direct attention receives it without leaving the remaining singers idle during that practice block.\nAll sections practice their assigned passages separately for the same short period and then return for a combined attempt. Each voice part receives focused attention before the conductor hears how the independently practiced lines fit together.\nSections take turns using a quiet corner for focused practice while the conductor rehearses compatible material with the remaining singers. Separate attention is staggered, allowing the conductor to keep useful ensemble work moving throughout the block."
    }
  ],
